[Music]  another afghan provincial capital just  south of kabul has fallen to the taliban  in an offensive that's shown few signs  of slowing the latest is sharana in the  eastern province of paktika videos from  the taliban appear to show the fighters  releasing inmates from a prison but as  they advance people are scrambling for  safety afghans are pouring into kabul  from areas captured by the taliban  fearful of living under taliban rule the  u.n is warning of an unfolding  humanitarian catastrophe  specifically reports  all right let's take you actually now to  afghanistan where ashwagandha the  president is speaking let's listen to  what he's saying to the problems of the  internal displaced people  national  security and defense forces  who are defending the nation  i'm thankful for them i'm thankful to  the  nation  our first priority is to bring  coordination  and swift actions in relation to  coordinating our  and dsf  our national security and defense forces  i would like to assure you  as your president  i know you are worried about the  situation we are going to  prevent  further displacement of the people  and  the situation for the people  i'm not going to allow this imposed war  on our nation to lead to more  bloodshed  and to  losing the gains  of our last two decades and to damages  to our  public goods and infrastructure because  of this  i've carried out  consultations widespread comprehensive  consultations with the representatives  of the people and leaders  and with our  international partners  consultations are being carried out  in a speedy manner i'm going to share  the results with you  quickly  your well-being your health  your success and prosperity are our  concerns our priorities we are going to  continue our efforts long live  afghanistan  let's go now live to rob mcbride in  kabul so rob there we heard quite a  defiant message from ashraf ghani put it  into context what is the bottom line of  his assurances to people  well yeah we've been all expecting this  speech from ashraf ghani he has become  increasingly embattled  by this crisis uh there has been an  awful lot of speculation that there  might be some kind of change in  leadership that he might announce even  possibly that he might be standing back  from government that's what the uh  speculation has been about leading up to  this speech uh the the the but as we've  heard there he seems to have recommitted  himself uh to carrying on the struggle  he  came on thanking the uh the the armed  forces for the sacrifices that they have  made he has uh talked about trying to  prevent further bloodshed and suffering  uh of the people he's spoken about not  giving up all of the advances and the  the the gains that have been made over  the last 20 years or so but that was it  that was the end of the speech and it's  very interesting because there has been  an awful lot of pressure on him that  there has to be some sort of political  solution to this it's not going to be  settled on the battlefield and the  political solution might involve him  stepping back and somebody else taking  over certainly that's been the demand  from  organizations like the taliban they will  have said repeatedly that if they're  going to talk about any power sharing  agreement then ashraf ghani is not going  to be part of that picture but as we've  heard there i don't know if this is  going to change anytime soon but for the  moment at least he seems committed  all right thanks so much to rob mcbride  there let's bring in victoria fonten  professor of peace studies at the  american university of afghanistan joins  us now on skype  from kabul good to have you with us if i  could start with a point that  president ashraf ghani mentioned there  without elaborating he did say  consultations are underway  what exactly do we understand that to  mean are these consultations and  negotiations for a transition to a  different government  well that could be the case uh because  this is something that the afghan  government has already proposed  a few days ago  and  and it could be uh that uh that this  solution could end uh the current  violence however uh the taliban have  asked for ashwagandha to step down  and have already rejected uh this type  of negotiated settlement so it remains  to be seen uh what happens with the  position of president ghani  it's so it has been suggested that the  uh  there is a mediation  effort underway led by ismail khan  to try and talk directly to the northern  alliance might that lead to a transition  transitional government that  does not include ashraf ghani as your as  you are pointing out there  uh well um th this this could well  happen i don't think that we have any  information to uh uh to speculate on  that uh at this time uh but again um uh  it's all about the current government  its legitimacy and uh and also the stand  that it is going to take in the next 24  hours so if this government um if the  president does not step down then does  it mean that  we have different political factions uh  um claiming uh to be uh the legitimate  rulers of afghanistan at in the middle  of a war uh nobody knows right now and  uh and i guess that uh the next hours  are going to be crucial the situation is  unfolding really rapidly here and  and there has been a lot of speculation  about the uh stepping down of president  ghani but so far  it all remains to be seen  overall the tone of president ashraf  ghani's message there was quite defiant  saying he will protect the people he  will not allow the gains of the last  couple of decades to be lost and so on  and so forth from your analysis though  with the latest gains made by the  taliban  can the government resist can it  withstand this taliban tide  i do not think it can  there is a lot of talk in kabul about  the impeding impending independent  states on thursday and many think that  the taliban are going to  go for kabul this week and declare  independence on thursday so i their  latest games are extremely significant  they're going for mazar sharif right now  and  and it's uh it's time for a negotiated  settlement uh  every minute every hour counts  and uh it's um i think that the  situation is so critical  uh those advances um are unlikely to  stop unless um the demands are being met  and that's uh extremely um unfortunate  what sort of posture do you expect from  the international community particularly  the us  um i don't quite know because  every time  they they indicate to the taliban not to  cross a certain boundary that boundary  is crossed and there is still uh  negotiations and uh and uh and there are  still uh agreements sought and there is  still a lot of uh in a way diplomatic  cajoling and so um i i i simply don't  know what to expect right now uh the uh  the focus is on  the evacuation of of embassy staff um  and i i think that the disengagement is  uh quite clear and has been clear for  some time and um and there is also from  that perspective uh a lot of defiance  and in transitions too and so in a  conflict where different parties are not  willing to budge then this of course  needs uh um  an open boulevard leaves an open  boulevard for the taliban to just  advance onto kabul and uh and and for  the winner to take it all really  all right thank you so much for your  analysis on that

